+    {
+      "key": "atomic",
+      "namespace": "analysis.spectral",
+      "logical_name": "annealed-first-poisson-moment-does-not-imply-quenched-ac",
+      "kind": "concept",
+      "title": "Annealed first moment can conceal atomic spectral measures",
+      "statement": "DRAFT concept backed by an author derivation, not a promoted theorem. For every lambda>0, the diagonal operator H delta_x=lambda V_x delta_x on ell^2(Z^3), with iid uniform[-1,1] V_x and no hopping, has atomic spectral measures for every realization. Yet sup_(E real,eta>0) E Im <delta_x,(H-E-i eta)^(-1)delta_x> <= pi/(2lambda). A bounded annealed first moment alone therefore does not imply quenched ac. This does not refute the Anderson operator with hopping.",
+      "proof_text": null,
+      "assumptions": [
+        "lambda>0",
+        "iid uniform[-1,1] site variables",
+        "diagonal operator with no hopping"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability and spectral theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-own-work:w487:c0056-boundary-20261010",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w487/ATTEMPT.md",
+        "revision": "campaign-author-2026-10-10-v2-nondegenerate-interval-fix",
+        "license": "owner-own-work (campaign derivation; independent/model gates separate)",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "criterion",
+      "namespace": "analysis.spectral",
+      "logical_name": "annealed-integrated-poisson-p-moment-ac-criterion",
+      "kind": "concept",
+      "title": "Sharp power-moment boundary for a quenched ac criterion",
+      "statement": "DRAFT concept backed by an author derivation, not a promoted theorem. Let mu_omega be random probability Borel measures, I a bounded open interval, 1<p<infinity, and eta_n>0 a deterministic sequence tending to zero. Set F_n(omega,E)=integral eta_n/((t-E)^2+eta_n^2) dmu_omega(t), jointly measurable. If sup_n E integral_I F_n^p <= C<infinity, then almost surely mu_omega|I=rho_omega dE with rho_omega in Lp(I), and E integral_I rho_omega^p <= C/pi^p. For 0<p<=1, integral_I F_eta^p <= |I|^(1-p)pi^p holds for every probability measure, including atoms; those power bounds alone cannot imply ac. p>1 is sufficient, not necessary for ac, and the criterion may yield zero mass on I.",
+      "proof_text": null,
+      "assumptions": [
+        "random probability Borel measures",
+        "bounded open I",
+        "deterministic eta_n -> 0",
+        "joint measurability",
+        "1<p<infinity for the sufficient criterion"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability and spectral theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-own-work:w487:c0056-boundary-20261010",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w487/ATTEMPT.md",
+        "revision": "campaign-author-2026-10-10-v2-nondegenerate-interval-fix",
+        "license": "owner-own-work (campaign derivation; independent/model gates separate)",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "second",
+      "namespace": "analysis.spectral",
+      "logical_name": "uniform-diagonal-imaginary-resolvent-second-moment-divergence",
+      "kind": "concept",
+      "title": "Atomic model has a divergent second imaginary-resolvent moment",
+      "statement": "DRAFT concept backed by an author derivation, not a promoted theorem. Let V be uniform[-1,1], lambda>0, eta>0 and F_eta=eta/((lambda V)^2+eta^2). Then E F_eta=arctan(lambda/eta)/lambda and E F_eta^2=1/(2(lambda^2+eta^2))+arctan(lambda/eta)/(2lambda eta). Consequently eta E F_eta^2 -> pi/(4lambda), while E F_eta -> pi/(2lambda), as eta -> 0 at fixed lambda.",
+      "proof_text": null,
+      "assumptions": [
+        "lambda>0 fixed",
+        "eta>0",
+        "V uniform[-1,1]"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability and spectral theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-own-work:w487:c0056-boundary-20261010",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w487/ATTEMPT.md",
+        "revision": "campaign-author-2026-10-10-v2-nondegenerate-interval-fix",
